ion and mutual assistance in criminal matters 1aWashington, D.C. :bLaw Library, Library of Congress,c1983.  a1 online resource (ii, 166 pages)  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.0 aIntroduction -- Structure and general principles of the ARHG -- Extradition -- Mutual assistance -- New forms of cooperation -- Appendix: Translation  a"On December 4, 1979, Austria enacted a Law on Extradition and Mutual Assistance in Criminal Matters (hereafter ARHG) that became effective on July 1, 1980. This legislation represented the first comprehensive statutory treatment of these matters for Austria, and the new law was needed, at least in part, to fill a sorely felt gap in the legal process, since the Austrian Constitution provides that all governmental acts must be based on statutory law"--Introduction, page 1.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title page (Law Library of Congress, viewed March 2, 2017).10aAustria.tAuslieferungs- und Rechtshilfegesetz.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n82233027 0aExtraditionzAustria. 0aJudicial assistancezAustria. 0aExtraditionzEurope. 0aJudicial assistancezEurope. 7aExtradition.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00919015 7aJudicial assistance.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00984636 7aAustria.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204901 7aEurope.2fast0(OCoLC)fst012450642 aLibrary of Congress.bLaw Library,0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n79055991eissuing body.08iPrint version:aPalmer, Edith, 1940-tAustrian law on extradition and mutual assistance in criminal mattersw(OCoLC)959479440uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo1398334 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://hdl.loc.gov/loc.law/llglrdppub.2019668774  3(online)uhttps://catalog.gpo.gov/fdlpdir/locate.jsp?ItemNumber=0818-J-06&SYS=001126617  aC0bGPO  aXZL4  aLCLAWLIB  aBIBCONEWb20200804  abc48 20200804b2020080401928nam a2200469 i 00112664320200805121605.0m